d K.G. Beam. <year>1985</year>b. Slow charge movement in mammalian skeletal muscle. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>85</volume>:<fpage>1</fpage>&#x02013;19.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3968530</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib47"><citation citation-type="journal">Stryer, L., and R.P. Haugland. <year>1967</year>. Energy transfer: a spectroscopic ruler. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>58</volume>:<fpage>719</fpage>&#x02013;726.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">5233469</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib48"><citation citation-type="journal">Tsien, R.Y., B.J. Bacskai, and S.R. Adams. <year>1993</year>. FRET for studying intracellular signalling. <source>Trends Cell Biol.</source> <volume>3</volume>:<fpage>242</fpage>&#x02013;245.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14731760</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib49"><citation citation-type="journal">Umeda, Y., T. Marui, Y. Matsuno, K. Shirahashi, H. Iwata, H. Takagi, K. Matsumoto, T. Nakamura, A. Kosugi, Y. Mori, and H. Takemura. <year>2004</year>. Skeletal muscle targeting in vivo electroporation-mediated HGF gene therapy of bleomycin-induced pulmonary fibrosis in mice. <source>Lab. Invest.</source> <volume>84</volume>:<fpage>836</fpage>&#x02013;844.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15197407</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib50"><citation citation-type="journal">Williams, A.T.R., S.A. Winfield, and J.N. Millar. <year>1983</year>. Relative fluorescence quantum yields using a computer-controlled luminescence spectrometer. <source>Analyst.</source> <volume>108</volume>:<fpage>1067</fpage>&#x02013;1071.</citation></ref><ref id="bib51"><citation citation-type="journal">Woods, C.E., D. Novo, M. DiFranco, and J.L. Vergara. <year>2004</year>. The action potential-evoked sarcoplasmic reticulum calcium release is impaired in mdx mouse muscle fibres. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>557</volume>:<fpage>59</fpage>&#x02013;75.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15004213</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib52"><citation citation-type="journal">Woods, C.E., D. Novo, M. DiFranco, J. Capote, and J.L. Vergara. <year>2005</year>. Propagation in the transverse tubular system and voltage dependence of calcium release in normal and mdx mouse muscle fibres. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>568</volume>:<fpage>867</fpage>&#x02013;880.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16123111</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib53"><citation citation-type="journal">Zhang, F.L., and P.J. Casey. <year>1996</year>. Protein prenylation: molecular mechanisms and functional consequences. <source>Annu. Rev. Biochem.</source> <volume>65</volume>:<fpage>241</fpage>&#x02013;269.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8811180</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><app-group><app id="app1"><title>APPENDIX</title><sec><title>Numerical Integration of the Radial Cable Model Equations for Mammalian Skeletal Muscle Fibers under Passive Conditions</title><p>We followed the nomenclature and procedures reported previously for amphibian muscle fibers (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib5">Ashcroft et al., 1985</xref>; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib31">Kim and Vergara, 1998b</xref>) and the assumption (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib1">Adrian and Peachey, 1973</xref>) that the lumen of the TTS is separated from the extracellular fluid by an access resistance (R<sub>s</sub>, in &#x003a9;cm<sup>2</sup>). The partial differential equation that governs the radial (<italic>r</italic>) and time (<italic>t</italic>) dependent changes in T-tubule potential, <italic>u</italic>(<italic>r,t</italic>) in response to potential changes at the external boundary is (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib2">Adrian et al., 1969</xref>)<disp-formula id="fda1"><label>(A1)</label><tex-math id="M19">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{{\partial}^{2}u}{{\partial}R^{2}}+\frac{1}{R}\frac{{\partial}u}{{\partial}R}={\nu}^{2}u+\frac{{\partial}u}{{\partial}t},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where <italic>a</italic> is the radius of the muscle fiber, <italic>R</italic> &#x0003d; <italic>r/a, T</italic> &#x0003d; <inline-formula><tex-math id="M20">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\bar {G}}_{L}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> t/(<inline-formula><tex-math id="M21">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\bar {C}}_{w}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> a<sup>2</sup>) and &#x003bd; &#x0003d; a<inline-formula><tex-math id="M22">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\sqrt{{\bar {G}}_{w}/{\bar {G}}_{L}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>. In these equations, the parameters <inline-formula><tex-math id="M23">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\bar {C}}_{w}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> and <inline-formula><tex-math id="M24">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\bar {G}}_{w}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> are the capacitance (in &#x003bc;F/cm<sup>3</sup>) and conductance (in S/cm<sup>3</sup>) of the tubular membrane per unit volume of muscle fiber, respectively, and <inline-formula><tex-math id="M25">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\bar {G}}_{L}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> is the effective radial conductivity (in S/cm). If &#x003c1; is the fraction of the total muscle volume occupied by T-tubules, &#x003c3; is a tortuosity factor, and &#x003be; is the volume to surface ratio of the T-tubules, then <inline-formula><tex-math id="M26">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\bar {C}}_{w}=C_{{\mathrm{W}}}{\mathrm{{\rho}}}/{\mathrm{{\xi}}};\hspace{.167em}{\bar {G}}_{w}={\mathrm{G}}_{{\mathrm{W}}}{\mathrm{{\rho}/{\xi}}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>; and, <inline-formula><tex-math id="M27">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\bar {G}}_{L}={\mathrm{G}}_{{\mathrm{L}}}{\mathrm{{\rho}{\sigma}}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>, where <italic>C<sub>w</sub></italic> and <italic>G<sub>w</sub></italic> are the TTS membrane capacitance (in &#x003bc;F/cm<sup>2</sup>) and conductance (in S/cm<sup>2</sup>) per unit surface membrane area, and <italic>G<sub>L</sub></italic> is the specific T-tubule lumen conductivity (in S/cm).</p><p>Numerical integration methods permit a more general approach to investigate the electrical properties of the TTS, and the radial cable equation can be changed in order to take into consideration the possibility that the TTS cable elements are parallel arrangements of nonlinear conductances and capacitances (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib1">Adrian and Peachey, 1973</xref>; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib29">Heiny et al., 1983</xref>; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib5">Ashcroft et al., 1985</xref>). Thus, we start by replacing the term <inline-formula><tex-math id="M28">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\partial}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula><sup>2</sup><italic>u</italic> in <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda1">Eq. A1</xref> by the more general term:<disp-formula><tex-math id="M29">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}a^{2}\frac{\overline{I_{W}}(V,r,t)}{\overline{G_{L}}},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where <inline-formula><tex-math id="M30">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\overline{I_{W}}({\mu},r,t)\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> is a generalized current through the TTS membrane wall normalized per volume of fiber. Keeping the same definitions for R and T, <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda1">Eq. A1</xref> is transformed into<disp-formula id="fda2"><label>(A2)</label><tex-math id="M31">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{{\partial}^{2}u}{{\partial}R^{2}}+\frac{1}{R}\frac{{\partial}u}{{\partial}R}=a^{2}\frac{\overline{I_{W}}}{\overline{G_{L}}}+\frac{{\partial}u}{{\partial}t}.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>For the simulations in this paper we assumed that <inline-formula><tex-math id="M32">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\overline{I_{W}}({\mu},r,t)\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> has resistive and capacitive components given by the equation<disp-formula id="fda3"><label>(A3)</label><tex-math id="M33">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\overline{I_{W}}({\mu},r,t)={\bar {G}}_{W}{\mu}+I_{Q}({\mu},r,t)\frac{{\rho}}{{\zeta}},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where <italic>I<sub>Q</sub></italic>(<italic>V</italic>, <italic>r</italic>, <italic>t</italic>) is the nonlinear charge movement current (in this case due to the presence of lipophilic ions) at every radial element of the TTS. Assuming a two-state model of the charge transition (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib21">Fernandez et al., 1983</xref>; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib45">Simon and Beam, 1985a</xref>), the following equations apply for every radial element of the TTS:<disp-formula id="fda4"><label>(A4)</label><tex-math id="M34">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}I_{Q}({\mu},r,t)=\frac{Q_{{\infty}}({\mu},r)-Q\hspace{.167em}({\mu},r)}{{\tau}({\mu},r)}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula><disp-formula id="fda5"><label>(A5)</label><tex-math id="M35">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}Q_{{\infty}}^{n}({\mu},r)=\frac{Q_{{\mathrm{max}}}}{1+e^{\displaystyle\frac{-{\alpha}F({\mu}-V_{h})}{RT}}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula><disp-formula id="fda6"><label>(A6)</label><tex-math id="M36">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tau}({\mu},r)=\frac{{\tau}_{{\mathrm{max}}}}{{\mathrm{cosh}} \left \left(\displaystyle\frac{{\alpha}F({\mu}-V_{h})}{2RT}\right) \right }.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>The TTS elements are approximated as isopotential concentric shells (<italic>s<sub>i</sub></italic>, with <italic>i</italic> ranging from 0 to <italic>n</italic>) each with voltage <italic>u<sub>i</sub></italic>. The simulations presented in the text were obtained with <italic>n</italic> &#x0003d; 60 shells; higher number of concentric annuli did not significantly alter the theoretical predictions.</p><p>At a given time <italic>j</italic>, the finite differences approximation of the partial differential equation of the T- tubular voltage is (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib13">Crank, 1975</xref>)<disp-formula><tex-math id="M37">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{{\partial}^{2}u}{{\partial}R^{2}}+\frac{1}{R}\frac{{\partial}u}{{\partial}R}{\rightarrow}\frac{1}{2i\hspace{.167em}{\delta}R^{2}}\{(2i+1)u_{i+1}^{j}-4iu_{i}^{j}+(2i-1)u_{i-1}^{j}\},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where <inline-formula><tex-math id="M38">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}u_{i}^{j}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> is the finite difference representation of the voltage at radial position <italic>i</italic> at time <italic>j</italic>, and &#x003b4;<italic>R</italic> is the normalized distance between shells. The TTS cable is assumed to be sealed at the center of the muscle fiber, and the approximation used to calculate <inline-formula><tex-math id="M39">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}u_{0}^{j}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> is (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib13">Crank, 1975</xref>)<disp-formula><tex-math id="M40">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{{\partial}^{2}u}{{\partial}R^{2}}+\frac{1}{R}\frac{{\partial}u}{{\partial}R}{\rightarrow}\frac{4}{{\delta}R^{2}}(u_{1}^{j}-u_{_{0}}^{j}).\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>The finite differences approximation for the time dependence partial differential operator for an arbitrary cable element <inline-formula><tex-math id="M41">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}u_{_{i}}^{j}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> is<disp-formula><tex-math id="M42">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{{\partial}u}{{\partial}T}{\rightarrow}\frac{u_{i}^{j+1}-u_{i}^{j}}{{\delta}T},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where &#x003b4;<italic>T</italic> is the dimensionless time interval. The external boundary condition (R &#x0003d; 1; <italic>i</italic> &#x0003d; <italic>n</italic>) is (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib1">Adrian and Peachey, 1973</xref>)<disp-formula><tex-math id="M43">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*} \left \left(\frac{{\partial}u}{{\partial}R}\right) \right _{R=1}=\frac{V_{COM}(t)-u_{n}^{j}}{aR_{s}{\bar {G}}_{L}},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where <italic>V<sub>COM</sub></italic>(<italic>t</italic>) is the pulse imposed at the surface membrane of the muscle fiber. The finite differences approximation for this boundary condition is given by<disp-formula><tex-math id="M44">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{u_{n}^{j}-u_{n-2}^{j}}{2{\delta}R}=\frac{V_{COM}-u_{n}^{l}}{aR_{s}{\bar {G}}_{L}}.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>Using an implicit Crank-Nicolson algorithm (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib13">Crank, 1975</xref>; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib23">Gerald, 1978</xref>), a final finite difference equation for an arbitrary annulus <italic>i</italic> can be obtained (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib31">Kim and Vergara, 1998b</xref>):<disp-formula><tex-math id="M45">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}{\mathrm{X}}{\cdot}\frac{(2{\mathrm{i}}+1)}{4{\mathrm{i}}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{u}}_{{\mathrm{i}}+1}^{{\mathrm{j}}}-({\mathrm{X}}+1){\cdot}{\mathrm{u}}_{{\mathrm{i}}}^{{\mathrm{j}}+1}+{\mathrm{X}}{\cdot}\frac{(2{\mathrm{i}}-1)}{4{\mathrm{i}}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{u}}_{{\mathrm{i}}-1}^{{\mathrm{j}}+1}= \\-{\mathrm{X}}{\cdot}\frac{(2{\mathrm{i}}+1)}{4{\mathrm{i}}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{u}}_{{\mathrm{i}}+1}^{{\mathrm{j}}}+({\mathrm{X}}-1){\cdot}{\mathrm{u}}_{i}^{j}-X{\cdot}\frac{(2{\mathrm{i}}-1)}{4{\mathrm{i}}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{u}}_{{\mathrm{i}}-1}^{{\mathrm{j}}}+a^{2}{\cdot}\frac{(\overline{I_{W}})_{i}^{j}}{\overline{G_{L}}}{\delta}T,\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>Where <inline-formula><tex-math id="M46">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}(\overline{I_{W}})_{i}^{j}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> is the current per unit fiber volume flowing through the T-tubular element at shell <italic>i</italic> and at the time interval <italic>j</italic> during the numerical integration process and X &#x0003d; &#x003b4;<italic>T</italic>/&#x003b4;<italic>R</italic><sup>2</sup>. This is a recursive formula allowing the calculation of <inline-formula><tex-math id="M47">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}u_{i}^{j+1}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> at a time interval &#x003b4;<italic>T</italic>, knowing <inline-formula><tex-math id="M48">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}u_{i}^{j}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>. The system of tridiagonal coefficient matrices was solved using an LU decomposition algorithm (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib23">Gerald, 1978</xref>). The integration of <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda3">Eq. A3</xref> to update the value of Q for every cable element was performed with a simple Euler algorithm at every time step. The values of the parameters used for the simulations shown in <xref rid="fig9" ref-type="fig">Fig. 9</xref> are summarized in <xref ref-type="table" rid="tbl3">Table III</xref>.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="tbl3"><label>TABLE III</label><caption><p>Specific Parameter Values for Passive Radial Model Simulations in <xref rid="fig9" ref-type="fig">Fig. 9</xref></p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">Parameter</th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">Value</th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">Dimension</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left"><italic>a</italic></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">30</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">&#x003bc;m</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">C<sub>W</sub></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">0.9</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">&#x003bc;F/cm<sup>2</sup></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left"><italic>G<sub>W</sub></italic></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">0.3</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">mS/ cm<sup>2</sup></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">GL</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">7</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">mS/cm</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">R<sub>s</sub></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">120</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">&#x003a9;cm<sup>2</sup></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">&#x003c1;</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">0.004</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top"/></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">&#x003b6;</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">1.1 &#x000d7; 10<sup>&#x02212;6</sup></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">cm</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">&#x003c3;</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">0.5</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top"/></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">Q<sub>max</sub></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">0.5</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">&#x003bc;C/cm<sup>2</sup></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">V<sub>h</sub></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">8</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">mV</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">&#x003b1;</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">0.84</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top"/></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">&#x003c4;<sub>max</sub> for DPA</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">0.6</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">ms</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">&#x003c4;<sub>max</sub> for DiBAC4(5)</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">1.6</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">ms</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><p>Under the assumption that the optical FRET interaction homogenously occurs at superficial and deep regions of the TTS, and that changes in its optical properties with the transmembrane voltage occur identically at every submicroscopic region of the TTS, then the optical signal obtained under global illumination is expected to represent an ensemble average of the translocated charge in the TTS (<inline-formula><tex-math id="M49">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\overline{{\Delta}Q_{TTS}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>) weighted by the radius of each annulus (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib29">Heiny et al., 1983</xref>; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib5">Ashcroft et al., 1985</xref>). This is calculated using a numerical trapezoidal integration algorithm based on Simpson's rule (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib23">Gerald, 1978</xref>) from the formula<disp-formula><tex-math id="M50">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\overline{{\Delta}V_{TTS}}=\frac{{\int _{0}^{a}}rV(r)dr}{{\int _{0}^{a}}rdr}.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>This assumption was used to calculate the predicted QRET signal (for comparison with the data) in <xref rid="fig9" ref-type="fig">Fig. 9 D</xref>.</p></sec></app></app-group></back></article>